	<section class="hero alt-hero">
		<div class="hero-badge">Alternatives · Self-Hosted</div>
		<h1>Stalwart alternative.<br/><span>Proven stack, transactional surface.</span></h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Stalwart is an impressive from-scratch Rust mail server — JMAP, CalDAV/CardDAV,
			clustering, pluggable storage, AI spam filtering. Vectis Mail takes a different bet:
			the battle-tested Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd stack, wrapped in a declarative single-file
			config, atomic updates with automatic rollback, a transactional sending API, and
			bundled webmail. This is an honest comparison of where each one wins.
		</p>
		<div class="alt-meta">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>14 June 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>Written by Ian Holt</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Side-by-side, current as of June 2026. Stalwart wins several rows — we call them out honestly.</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Vectis Mail</th><th>Stalwart</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mail engine</th><td><strong>Postfix · Dovecot · Rspamd</strong> (decades-hardened)</td><td>From-scratch Rust (all protocols reimplemented)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Footprint</th><td>Container stack (Postgres, Traefik, API)</td><td><strong>Single Rust binary</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Protocols</th><td>SMTP · IMAP · POP3</td><td><strong>SMTP · IMAP · POP3 · JMAP · CalDAV · CardDAV · WebDAV</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Calendar / contacts</th><td>Not yet (roadmap)</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Webmail</th><td><strong>Roundcube bundled</strong></td><td>None (bring your own, e.g. Roundcube)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Config model</th><td><strong>Declarative <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>vectis apply</code></strong></td><td>TOML files + web admin</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Transactional / programmatic API</th><td>REST sending API — domain-scoped keys, batch, attachments</td><td>JMAP + REST admin API</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Webhooks</th><td>Yes — HMAC-signed inbound + delivery events</td><td>Yes — event webhooks + MTA Hooks</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Spam filtering</th><td>Rspamd (mature, scriptable)</td><td>Built-in — statistical + AI/LLM, Pyzor, blocklists</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Antivirus</th><td>ClamAV (bundled, optional)</td><td>SMTP-time filtering; AV via integration</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Clustering / HA</th><td>Single-node</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — node autodiscovery, partition-tolerant</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Storage</th><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><strong>Pluggable</strong> — RocksDB, FoundationDB, Postgres, S3, …</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Update model</th><td><strong>Atomic 6-phase orchestrator</strong> with auto-rollback</td><td>Binary / package upgrade</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">License</th><td>BSL 1.1 (auto-Apache 2.0 after 4y) + commercial Pro</td><td>AGPL-3.0 + SELv1 (Enterprise)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Commercial tier</th><td>Pro $39/tenant/mo · Enterprise from $499 + SLA</td><td>Enterprise edition (paid)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Maturity</th><td>v0.1.x — production since April 2026; proven components</td><td>Modern, rapidly evolving, from-scratch codebase</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The verdict</h2>
		<div class="verdict-grid">
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-win">
				<h3>Choose Vectis Mail when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>You'd rather run battle-tested Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd than a from-scratch reimplementation of every protocol.</li>
					<li>You want one declarative <code>config.yaml</code> and atomic updates with automatic rollback.</li>
					<li>You're building a transactional sending platform — domain-scoped API keys, per-domain analytics, IP-warmup tracking — with mailboxes on the same stack.</li>
					<li>You want bundled Roundcube webmail rather than wiring up a separate client.</li>
					<li>You want a vendor-backed Pro / Enterprise tier with a support SLA.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-stay">
				<h3>Choose Stalwart when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>You need JMAP, or built-in calendar/contacts (CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV) groupware.</li>
					<li>You want built-in clustering / high availability.</li>
					<li>You want pluggable, distributed storage (FoundationDB, S3, Elasticsearch, …).</li>
					<li>You prefer a single self-contained binary over a container stack.</li>
					<li>You like the idea of the whole stack in one modern, memory-safe Rust codebase.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Philosophy: proven components vs from-scratch</h3>
		<p>
			This is the core difference. Stalwart reimplements SMTP, IMAP, POP3, JMAP and the DAV
			protocols from the ground up in Rust — one cohesive, memory-safe codebase with no
			Postfix or Dovecot underneath. That's genuinely impressive and gives Stalwart a clean,
			unified surface. The trade-off is that it is a younger implementation of protocols that
			Postfix and Dovecot have been hardening in production for two decades.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail makes the opposite bet: keep the boring, proven mail engine
			(Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd) and put the modern surface — Go API, React admin, declarative
			config, orchestrated updates — <em>around</em> it. If "my SMTP daemon has handled
			internet mail since before the company existed" reads as a feature to you, that's the
			Vectis argument in one line.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Protocols and groupware</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is broader here, and we won't pretend otherwise. It speaks JMAP (the modern
			JSON mail protocol) plus CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV, so calendar and contacts are part
			of the server. Vectis Mail today is SMTP/IMAP/POP3 only; CalDAV/CardDAV is on the
			roadmap, not shipped. If groupware is a hard requirement right now, Stalwart covers it
			and Vectis doesn't.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">The transactional API</h3>
		<p>
			Both have programmatic surfaces — this isn't a Vectis-only feature. Stalwart offers
			JMAP for submission/access, a REST admin API, and event webhooks plus MTA Hooks.
			Vectis Mail's difference is framing and packaging: a dedicated transactional sending
			API with <strong>domain-scoped API keys</strong>, batch sending with attachments,
			per-domain rate limits and analytics, and HMAC-signed inbound webhooks — presented as a
			self-hosted SendGrid/Postmark replacement rather than a JMAP server. If your mental
			model is "give my app an API key and a send endpoint," Vectis maps to it directly; if
			it's "give my client a JMAP account," Stalwart does.
		</p>
		<p>See the <a href="/api/">API Reference</a> for the full Vectis surface.</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Config and updates</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is configured through TOML files and its Rust/Leptos web admin, and upgrades
			are a binary/package replacement. Vectis Mail uses a single <code>config.yaml</code> as
			the source of truth: <code>vectis apply</code> diffs desired against running state and
			reloads only what changed, and updates run through a 6-phase orchestrator
			(snapshot → migrate → pull → deploy → health-check → complete) that <strong>automatically
			rolls back</strong> on any failure. If config-as-code and safe, reversible updates
			matter to you, that's a Vectis advantage.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Scale and storage</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is built to scale out: node autodiscovery, partition-tolerant failure
			detection, and pluggable storage backends from RocksDB to FoundationDB, S3 and
			Elasticsearch. Vectis Mail is a single-node design on PostgreSQL — deliberately simple,
			and the right shape for the one-VPS-per-deployment model most self-hosters run, but not
			a clustered HA system. If you need multi-node HA, Stalwart is the stronger fit.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Stalwart to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			Migration is standard IMAP-to-IMAP, since both expose IMAP regardless of what's
			underneath:
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS via the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>, with DNS still pointed at Stalwart.</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mailboxes</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> from Stalwart's IMAP to Vectis Mail's Dovecot IMAP.</li>
			<li><strong>Generate new DKIM keys</strong> on Vectis Mail and publish them alongside the existing ones; run both in parallel during the cutover.</li>
			<li><strong>Re-create Sieve filters</strong> as needed (both support server-side Sieve).</li>
			<li><strong>Export calendar/contacts</strong> from Stalwart (CalDAV/CardDAV) to iCal/vCard if you use them — Vectis can't host these, so plan where they'll live.</li>
			<li><strong>Cut DNS over</strong>: switch MX to Vectis Mail and watch it take over as DNS propagates.</li>
			<li><strong>Verify + decommission</strong> after a few clean days.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			The honest caveat: if you rely on Stalwart's JMAP clients or built-in calendar/contacts,
			there's no equivalent on Vectis yet, so that part of a migration is a feature change,
			not a lift-and-shift.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Stalwart better than Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					Neither is strictly better — they make different bets. Stalwart is broader on
					protocols (JMAP, CalDAV, CardDAV, WebDAV), ships built-in clustering and pluggable
					distributed storage, and reimplements spam filtering with an AI/statistical
					classifier. Vectis Mail packages the battle-tested Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd stack
					with a declarative single-file config, atomic updates with automatic rollback, a
					transactional sending API, bundled Roundcube webmail, and a commercial Pro/SLA
					option. Pick Stalwart for groupware, JMAP and HA; pick Vectis for proven
					components, declarative ops and a transactional platform.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Stalwart have a transactional sending API and webhooks?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Stalwart exposes JMAP for programmatic submission and access, a REST admin
					API, and event webhooks (plus MTA Hooks, an HTTP-based milter replacement).
					Vectis Mail's difference is packaging: domain-scoped API keys, batch sending with
					attachments, per-domain rate limits and analytics, and HMAC-signed inbound
					webhooks presented as a hosted-it-yourself SendGrid/Postmark replacement rather
					than a JMAP server.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Stalwart include webmail?</h3>
				<p>
					No. Stalwart doesn't ship a webmail client — you connect an external client or
					deploy something like Roundcube alongside it. Vectis Mail bundles Roundcube
					webmail with ManageSieve filter management out of the box.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Stalwart support calendar and contacts (CalDAV/CardDAV)?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes — Stalwart speaks CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV alongside JMAP/IMAP/POP3/SMTP, so
					calendar and contacts are built in. Vectis Mail doesn't ship CalDAV/CardDAV yet
					(it's on the roadmap), so if groupware is a hard requirement today, that's the
					clearest point in Stalwart's favour.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>What does Vectis Mail offer that Stalwart doesn't?</h3>
				<p>
					Battle-tested components (Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd rather than an all-new
					implementation), a single declarative <code>config.yaml</code> with atomic 6-phase
					updates and automatic rollback, bundled Roundcube webmail, IP-warmup tracking and
					per-domain analytics on Pro, and a vendor-backed Pro ($39/tenant/month) and
					Enterprise (from $499) tier with a support SLA. Stalwart also has a paid Enterprise
					edition, so commercial support exists on both sides.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

